<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Toll-Free Verification (TFV)</h2>
	<p>In addition to 10DLC, businesses sending A2P messaging from <strong>toll-free numbers</strong> (800, 888, 877, 866, 855, 844, 833 prefixes) must complete <strong>Toll-Free Verification</strong> through their messaging provider or the verifying carrier. Toll-free verification is a separate compliance path from 10DLC — the two systems do not overlap.</p>
	<p>Unverified toll-free numbers may be blocked or filtered by carriers. Verified toll-free numbers receive higher throughput and improved deliverability.</p>
	<h3>TFV requirements</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>Business name, website, and contact information must be consistent.</li>
		<li>Use case must be described accurately.</li>
		<li>Opt-in method and disclosure must be documented.</li>
		<li>Sample messages must match the described use case.</li>
		<li>Privacy policy and terms must be live and accessible.</li>
		<li>Consent evidence must be available for review.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>The same consent, disclosure, STOP/HELP, and content rules described above apply to toll-free messaging. The difference is the registration path: toll-free verification is managed through the verifying carrier or aggregator rather than through TCR.</p>
	<p>Choose 10DLC when you need local number presence and campaign-level registration. Choose toll-free verification when your use case is customer-service oriented, OTP, or notification-based traffic from a toll-free number.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Special campaign types</h2>

	<h3>Political messaging</h3>
	<p>Political messaging is a distinct TCR campaign use case with additional requirements. Political campaigns must:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Register under the political campaign use case in TCR.</li>
		<li>Comply with FEC regulations and applicable state election laws.</li>
		<li>Identify the political committee or organization paying for the message.</li>
		<li>Include "Paid for by [committee name]" in the message or disclosure.</li>
		<li>Obtain express written consent specific to political messaging.</li>
		<li>Not use purchased, rented, or shared voter lists as the sole consent source.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Political messaging is subject to heightened carrier scrutiny. Some carriers restrict or prohibit certain political content categories.</p>

	<h3>Charity and nonprofit messaging</h3>
	<p>Charities and nonprofits must still register brands and campaigns through TCR. Nonprofit status does not exempt an organization from 10DLC registration, consent, or disclosure requirements. Specific considerations include:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Register the legal entity name as it appears on the IRS determination letter or state registration.</li>
		<li>Select the appropriate campaign use case (e.g., charitable solicitation, donor updates).</li>
		<li>Comply with state charitable solicitation laws where applicable.</li>
		<li>Obtain consent specific to the messaging program — event attendance alone is not consent to receive ongoing texts.</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Age-gated content</h3>
	<p>Campaigns involving alcohol, tobacco, cannabis (where state-legal), or other age-restricted products require additional controls:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Age verification at the point of opt-in (e.g., date-of-birth field or age-gate page).</li>
		<li>Carrier pre-approval before sending — some carriers require advance review of age-gated campaigns.</li>
		<li>Clear disclosure that the content is intended for adults of legal age.</li>
		<li>Compliance with applicable federal, state, and local laws governing the product.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Federally illegal cannabis promotion remains prohibited regardless of state law. State-legal cannabis messaging may be permitted by some carriers with additional restrictions, age gating, and carrier pre-approval — but it carries elevated risk of filtering or rejection.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Campaign trust scores and throughput</h2>
	<p>TCR assigns each registered brand a <strong>trust score</strong> based on the completeness and accuracy of the brand verification. The trust score, combined with the campaign use case and vetting level, determines the <strong>daily message throughput</strong> allotted by each carrier.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Unverified brands</strong> cannot register campaigns or send on 10DLC.</li>
		<li><strong>Verified brands</strong> receive baseline throughput. Throughput varies by carrier and use case.</li>
		<li><strong>Vetted brands</strong> (standard or enhanced vetting) may receive higher throughput tiers. Enhanced vetting involves a deeper review and typically takes 2–4 weeks.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Throughput is managed at the number/campaign level (AT&amp;T) or brand level (T-Mobile). Businesses with high-volume needs should review throughput requirements before filing and consider vetting to access higher tiers.</p>
	<p>Consistently operating within your allotted throughput, maintaining low complaint rates, and keeping traffic aligned with the registered use case all contribute to sustained or improved throughput over time.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>International messaging considerations</h2>
	<p>The compliance standards on this page focus on <strong>U.S. messaging requirements</strong> (TCPA, CTIA, TCR, FCC). Businesses sending messages to recipients outside the United States must also comply with the messaging laws of each destination country.</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Canada:</strong> Canada's Anti-Spam Legislation (CASL) requires express or implied consent for commercial electronic messages, including SMS. Canada does not currently use the 10DLC registration system; toll-free and long-code messaging follow different carrier rules.</li>
		<li><strong>European Union / UK:</strong> GDPR and the ePrivacy Directive require consent for marketing messages. Each EU member state may have additional national rules. The UK follows PECR (Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations).</li>
		<li><strong>Other jurisdictions:</strong> Many countries have their own consent, disclosure, and content requirements for commercial messaging.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Customers sending internationally should consult legal counsel familiar with each destination market and confirm carrier requirements with VoiceTel before launching.</p>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Best practices for compliance</h2>
	<p>To maintain healthy message delivery and avoid carrier filtering:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Obtain documented consent before messaging</li>
		<li>Use prior express written consent for marketing messages</li>
		<li>Clearly identify your brand</li>
		<li>Keep content relevant and expected</li>
		<li>Include STOP instructions where required</li>
		<li>Honor opt-outs immediately and send only one confirmation message</li>
		<li>Register all A2P campaigns properly</li>
		<li>Complete toll-free verification if sending from toll-free numbers</li>
		<li>Use branded domains in links</li>
		<li>Avoid misleading urgency or deceptive language</li>
		<li>Respect quiet hours (8:00 AM – 9:00 PM local time for marketing)</li>
		<li>Scrub marketing lists against the National Do Not Call Registry</li>
		<li>Retain consent records for at least four years</li>
		<li>Monitor complaint and opt-out rates</li>
		<li>Consider double opt-in for stronger consent evidence</li>
	</ul>
</section>

